<Layout
    title="About"
    description="Geophysicist who writes software and takes both too seriously. Occasionally on purpose."
    pubDate={new Date('March 19 2026')}
>
    <p>
        By day, I process GNSS data and build headless automation pipelines for geohazard assessments. That's the one place where taking everything this seriously is correct. Outside of work, I build full-stack apps and local-first systems for startups and freelance clients.
    </p>

    <p>
        This page used to say my "engineering philosophy is entirely driven by frustration and constraints." In practice, something annoys me and I build far too much of a fix. A B2B lab marketplace, a constraint engine for math worksheets, and an autocorrect for a <a href="/blog/dead-i-key/">keyboard I could have replaced</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The first post on this blog explained, at length, that AI tools don't democratize outcomes. It was mostly right and very hard to read, so I <a href="/blog/luddite/">rewrote it</a> with jokes.
    </p>

    <p>
        Most posts here will be me catching myself overdoing something. When a post is serious, it's because it's about something I know well or decided to take seriously, and I'll say which.
    </p>
</Layout>
